  "note": "A Bde defending a ditch cum bund (DCB), GSP 1947 Inf Div in Battle s26 pp 118-121: normally all three Bns are deployed three up and the depth is given from within their own resources (para 8 b pp 120-121); a Bde may be assigned up to 8000-10000 m depending on the terrain and the allotment of second line troops (para 5 p 119). The Bns are laid as a Bn along an AT ditch is (IBB Annex V App 9 p 485: 2750-4115 m, the lower end here, 1850 m deep, each with its own depth Coy and second line force; built as Bn plans with the Bn Def AT ditch layout), 450 m apart (the App 9 gap between Coys; no gap between Bns is printed, E), so the Bde holds 9.15 km of the obstacle. The gaps are covered by artillery fire, automatics, AT fire, mines and patrols (para 8 c p 121): mines in each gap and on the far side of the ditch (App 9). Because of the extended frontage, prepared contingency (counter-penetration) posns are made in depth astride his major axes (para 8 f p 121): two, behind the gaps, drawn planned (dashed), 1 km behind the Bns (cpen.behindFwdM.bde, E); with the terrain they belong astride the approaches. Surveillance and protective elements are sited ahead of the obstacle if the terrain and space permit (para 6 p 120): a screen 1.5 km ahead of the ditch (E: DCBs lie 1-6 km from the border, p 118). Strong mobile reserves for counter-penetration and counter-attack are the Div's (para 6 p 120; para 8 g p 121), not the Bde's. He assaults a DCB at two or three points, aiming to finish in one night or at most two (para 8 d-e p 121). The ditch is the ground's, never drawn: click the point on the ditch in front of the centre and aim across it. Bde HQ centrally in depth and an AT Res between the contingency posns (OOW1 s13 p 45), both places E.",
